THUMB-1 ASSEMBLY DEBUGGING REFERENCE

The on-device 'as' is a tiny two-pass assembler for RP2040 Thumb-1.
This is not GNU as. Refer to /man/languages/asm.

WORKFLOW
  write_file('/tmp/t.s', 'movs r0, #3\nmovs r1, #0\nsvc #0\n')
  as /tmp/t.s
  hexdump /tmp/a.out 0 32
  /tmp/a.out
Output includes 8-byte PNX1 header; program code follows.

REGISTER/INSTRUCTION RULES
  Most data-processing operands are r0..r7. PC-relative addressing
  is restricted; 'bx lr', 'push {r4,lr}', 'pop {r4,pc}' accepted.
  movs r0, #255 is supported; a 32-bit immediate usually needs
  multiple instructions. No movw/movt or literal pools.
  svc #0 enters kernel (syscall number in r0, args in r1..r3).
  Branches have Thumb-1 distance limits and labels <=32.
  Comments use @ or ;. Label and directive syntax is restricted.

READING 'invalid instruction, label or size'
  Check spelling and accepted instruction forms.
  Check .align 2 for ADR's word-aligned forward data target.
  Check memory offsets: word accesses aligned and scaled.
  Check missing label and duplicate label.
  Check source <=2048 bytes and emitted code <=2040 bytes.

POSITION INDEPENDENCE
  ADR and relative labels are preferred over absolute references.
  Code must be valid in any user slot, not only 0x2000e000.
  The host build PIC test compares two linked addresses for
  native user binaries; on-device code should follow the same rule.

SYSCALL NUMBERS
  See /man/asm-examples and actual include/piconix_syscall.h.
  Do not invent syscalls from Linux's ARM EABI table.
